{"name":"Crystal Yellow","hex":"#e4d99f","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"crystal","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A solid composed of an array of atoms or molecules possessing long-range order and arranged in a pattern which is periodic in three dimensions.","A piece of glimmering, shining mineral resembling ice or glass."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Made of crystal.","Very clear; coherent."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/crystal"},{"term":"yellow","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The color of sunflower petals and lemons; the color obtained by mixing green and red light, or by subtracting blue from white light; the color evoked by light of wavelength around 580 nm; one of the three primary colors in subtractive color systems.","The middle light in a set of three traffic lights, the lighting of which indicates that drivers should stop short of the intersection if it is safe to do so."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a yellow hue.","Lacking courage."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/yellow"}],"entries":[{"title":"Crystal","blocks":[{"text":"A crystal or crystalline solid is a solid material whose constituents are arranged in a highly ordered microscopic structure, forming a crystal lattice that extends in all directions. In addition, macroscopic single crystals are usually identifiable by their geometrical shape, consisting of flat faces with specific, characteristic orientations. The scientific study of crystals and crystal formation is known as crystallography. The process of crystal formation via mechanisms of crystal growth is called crystallization or solidification."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8c/Quartz_28.jpg/330px-Quartz_28.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Crystal"},{"title":"Yellow","blocks":[{"text":"Yellow is a color between green and orange on the spectrum of light. It is evoked by light with a dominant wavelength of roughly 575–585 nm. It is a primary color in subtractive color systems, used in painting or color printing. In the RGB color model, used to create colors on television and computer screens, yellow is a secondary color made by combining red and green at equal intensity. Carotenoids give the characteristic yellow color to autumn leaves, corn, canaries, daffodils, and lemons, as well as egg yolks, buttercups, and bananas. They absorb light energy and protect plants from photo damage in some cases. Sunlight has a slight yellowish hue when the Sun is near the horizon, due to atmospheric scattering of shorter wavelengths."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d5/Cornwall_Daffodils.jpg/330px-Cornwall_Daffodils.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Yellow"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e9/Sodium-chloride-3D-ionic.png/500px-Sodium-chloride-3D-ionic.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e9/Sodium-chloride-3D-ionic.png/500px-Sodium-chloride-3D-ionic.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ASodium-chloride-3D-ionic.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7c/Selpologne.jpg/500px-Selpologne.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7c/Selpologne.jpg/1280px-Selpologne.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7c/Selpologne.jpg/500px-Selpologne.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org